-origin cross-validation.\n- Evaluate with metrics: RMSE, MAE, MAPE, directional accuracy.\n- Compare against the naive baseline; keep only if it adds value.\n\n## Step 7: Generate the Forecast\n- Produce a point estimate for the next trading day's close.\n- Compute a confidence/prediction interval (e.g., 80% or 95%).\n- Optionally provide a directional call (appreciate/depreciate) with probability.\n\n## Step 8: Sanity Check & Contextualize\n- Cross-check against PBOC fixing and overnight offshore CNH rate.\n- Flag any major scheduled events that could invalidate the forecast.\n- Note model limitations (FX is near-random-walk short-term).\n\n## Step 9: Present the Output\n- Deliver: forecast value, interval, key drivers, and assumptions.\n- Include a short methodology summary and data timestamp.\n- Add a clear disclaimer: not financial advice; forecasts are probabilistic.\n\n## Step 10: Monitor & Update\n- Re-run the model after new data (e.g., next PBOC fixing).\n- Track forecast error to refine the model over time.\n\n---\n\n**Key caveat:** Short-horizon FX forecasting is inherently noisy; even good models often barely beat a random walk. The plan emphasizes transparency, validation, and probabilistic output rather than false precision."}
